Article 15
Forms of services for victims organized by PPT include:
a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medicolegal services constitute a form of medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
c. psychosocial services constitute services provided in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims, including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from various threats and intimidation for victims and to provide social support so that victims have self-confidence, strength and independence in resolving their problems;
d. legal services to assist victims in undergoing legal processes; and
e. economic independence services in the form of skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.

Article 21
(1) Forms of services provided to women and children victims of acts of violence are as follows:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid;
d. return;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. identification services; and
g. psychological services.
(2) Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by SKPD and UKPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual.
(3) In the implementation of duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the regional government cooperates with government agencies, the provincial government, other regency/city governments, and the community.
(4) Further provisions regarding procedures for services and handling of women and children victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3) shall be regulated by a Mayor's Regulation.

CHAPTER III
RIGHTS OF VICTIMS
Article 4
Every victim has the right to obtain:
a. protection;
b. information;
c. optimal services;
d. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. confidential handling;
f. psychological and legal assistance; and
g. guarantee of rights related to status as a family member as well as a member of society.

Article 5
Child victims of acts of violence, in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in Article 4, also obtain special rights, as follows:
a. the right to full respect and full fulfillment for survival, growth, and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and
e. the right to obtain freedom.

Article 20
The provision of services and assistance to victims shall be conducted with the principles of:
a. fast, safe, and empathetic;
b. guarantee of confidentiality;
c. easily accessible; and
d. free of charge.

Article 3
(1) The objectives of implementing protection of women and children from acts of violence are to:
a. prevent acts of violence against women and children including human trafficking;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. provide services to women and children victims of acts of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and
e. facilitate and mediate household disputes to realize the integrity of harmonious and prosperous households.
(2) The objectives of implementing protection of women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), include aspects of:
a. prevention;
b. services and assistance;
c. reunification; and
d. empowerment.

Article 25
(1) In order to achieve the objectives of implementing protection for women and children from acts of violence as referred to in Article 3, the regional government cooperates with:
a. the government;
b. the provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments; and
d. non-governmental institutions.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of physical evidence and witnesses, and shall be followed up in accordance with applicable law.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), shall be set forth in the form of a joint agreement.

Article 23
(1) The Regional Government is obligated to carry out empowerment of women victims of acts of violence.
(2) Empowerment of victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by P2TP2A.
(3) In carrying out empowerment of women victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government shall formulate empowerment programs in the economic field, which include:
 a. endeavoring to provide the needs required for entrepreneurship training for women victims of acts of violence, in order to increase knowledge, attitudes, and business skills;
 b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;
 c. providing assistance in developing productive economic enterprises;
 d. exploring cooperation with small, medium, and large enterprises, as well as financial institutions, to develop the businesses of women victims of acts of violence;
 e. endeavoring to provide capital for women victims of acts of violence; and
 f. expanding access to information and promoting the results of the products of women victims of acts of violence.
(4) In carrying out empowerment of women victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government may establish cooperation with job training institutions organized by the private sector.

Article 17
(1) To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, the regional government conducts empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government undertakes efforts as follows:
a. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
